     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 9,000 mg/day of acetaminophen is in the range that can cause acute liver injury in a 70 kg adult. For comparison, about 150 mg/kg (roughly 10.5 g) in 24 hours is the usual toxicity threshold. I don't know of a clinical situation where this regimen is appropriate, and I can't enter an order that I expect to harm the patient.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the safe range right away.** For example, 1000 mg PO three or four times daily. A lower dose may also be enough for the pain or fever you're treating.\n- **Help you reach pharmacy.** If you think there's a special reason for this dose, a pharmacist or toxicology can review it before anything is ordered.\n- **Help with other options.** If acetaminophen alone isn't controlling symptoms, I can enter an adjunct or alternative agent.\n\nTell me which you'd like."
      }
     ],
